// expect-error-at: 8241:28
mod:list_ptr_for_binding;
// A `for` BINDING'S VALUE IS READ OUT OF ITS SOURCE (DEF-595, landing 177d2b3). `for (H->:m in pa)` over a parameter's storage of pointers gave `m` the source's refs
// as the parameter's own value, and the write side's reader answered nothing for a pointer whose root is no local. So `int64->:e = raw fp(m); discard(raw growh(m));`
// wrote to no one and `e` read freed heap, where the same take and growth by index (`pa[1i64]`) were refused. Now the binding's refs take the binding's type as their
// read, as `pa[i]`'s do (DEF-584's mark), and a pointer rooted at a `for` binding is resolved by its value's refs, each asked as a target. Each module is one cell.
// `zf`: the parameter's storage: an array (`zf1`), through a pointer to it (`zf2`), a slice (`zf3`), a member array (`zf4` to `zf6`), an array of pointers to structs or of
// structs with a pointer member (`zf7` to `zf9`), two loops deep (`zf10`), a local copy of the binding (`zf11`), a range of the array (`zf13`). `tg` takes and grows
// through the binding, `tb` takes through the binding and grows by index, `ti` the reverse, `ii` does both by index (the yardstick, refused before this row).
// `zl`: the container is a local of the iterating frame: of two pointer parameters' values (`zl2`), a copy of the parameter's array (`zl3`); `zl1`, of this frame's own
// addresses, is BORROW-009 at the loop. `zp`: a `pick` binding's twins, refused by the selector's freeze. `zr4`, `zt5`, `yf`, `yt8`: the same reader met in other rows' cells.
// `zh7` to `zh9`: the ADDRESS of a local copy of the binding, of a local read out by index and of the parameter's own element, read through (`HP->:k = @c; … k.lp`).
// `zm`: the mark's reach: a parameter's value and the binding in one literal (`zm1k`), a `pick` inside a `for` and a `for` inside a `pick` (`zm3k`, `zm4k`), a binding
// that is a slice of pointers (`zm5`), and the local twins of a binding assigned in the body (`zm2l`, `zm2ln`, `zm2fl`, `zm2al`, `zm2p`, `zm2a`).
// `zu1k`, `zu2k`: each is accepted by one mutant of this row's text and faults there. `zu1k`: a value read out of a parameter and pushed BEFORE the binding in one
// literal keeps its own read (`TW{ hp: q.lp, gp: m }`; `zu1r` is the other order). `zu2k`: what was stored INTO the binding through its address keeps the read it was
// stored with (an `any->` member's value, carried on as a `G->`). `zu4k`: the growth through the binding under 2, 30, 32 and 34 casts.
// `zu2s`, `zu2t`, `zu2u`, `zu3k`, `zu3n`: a pointer parameter stored into the binding through its address, then the take and the growth through the binding and the
// parameter. `zu8k`, `zu8v`: the iterable a callee's slice view of a parameter's array of pointers, and a range of it. `zu9o`: a local array of addresses of two types.
// `zd`: DEPTH. This row's reader and the others that read a value's refs for the aliasing walk (`escape_value_views`) walked with the carry fuel, 32 nodes, and answered
// nothing past it, where the statement that took the view walks with the escape walk's bound, 256. So a write through a `for` binding that was refused when shallow was
// accepted when deep, and read freed heap. `zd1`: a member chain on the binding (`m.s.….s.hp`; 30 steps were refused, 31 and 63 were not). `zd2`: the iterable a member
// chain (`for (H->:m in q.s.….s.arr)`; 30, 31, 70). `zd3`: loops nested over member arrays of the binding above (13, 16, 33). `k` takes and grows through the binding,
// `w` takes by index and grows through it (the side that was blind), `t` the reverse and `m` a local copy of the pointer (never blind).
// `zd5`: the other reader that moved, with no `for` binding: a call's result that stands as an argument while a later argument grows the `List` (`rd2(raw fp(X), raw
// growh(@h))`, DEF-575's party), or as an assignment's target (`<-(raw fp(X)) = raw growh(@h);`), `X` the address under 28 to 66 casts. Past 30 casts (28 for the target)
// no party was raised. Past the bound itself (three loops over 85-step chains) the walk still ends silently, on the take side too: DEF-606, open.
// `zd7t_pile_60_loops2_chain100`: the growth through the binding under 60 casts, two loops over 100-step chains. The reader takes the pointer UNDER its casts, so the casts
// don't count against the walk's bound; read with them the walk is past it, and the program is accepted and reads freed heap.
// TWO RIGHT PROGRAMS ARE REFUSED HERE, THE ROW'S NAMED PRICES.
// `zn3c`: the binding is a by-value struct of two pointers of two types, the view through one member and the growth through the other. Its source is one root, as it is
// for the same read by index (`zn3i`), through a by-value parameter (`zn3v`), through a pointer parameter (`zn2c`) and in one frame (`zn2l`), each refused before this row.
// `zu3c` (DEF-605, open): a pointer parameter stored into a member of the binding THROUGH THE BINDING'S ADDRESS, a view through that parameter, and the growth through the
// binding's OTHER member, of another type. The write's reader walks the pointer expression, so the member read gives the stored pointer the member's type. ITS TWINS ARE
// ACCEPTED: over a local copy of the element (`zu3l`) and over a by-value parameter (`zu3v`) the same program runs right, in `list_ptr_for_binding_ok.npk`.
// `zn3l` (a local copy of the element, two types) is DEF-590's price, refused since that row and named here: its direct spellings are `zn3i`, `zn3v` and `zn2l`.
// `zn4c` (the `pick` binding's twin of `zn3c`) is refused on every build by the selector's freeze.
// TWO CELLS ARE REFUSED BY ANOTHER RULE, ON EVERY BUILD, AND STAND HERE AS TRIPWIRES. A pointer rooted at a `for` binding is resolved without DEF-426's hop (what an address
// handed to a call holds), which a local's reader has. No accepted program reaches the difference: a store of an address through the binding is BORROW-002 (`zu6k`), and
// an iterable that holds a borrow of this frame is BORROW-009 at the loop (`zu5s`). If either refusal is ever relaxed, the hop is owed at the binding's root.
mod:yf2d_for_param_array = {
error:E5_yf2d_for_param_array;
struct:K = { int64:k; };
struct:H = { List<int64>:l; int64:k; K:kk; };
struct:H2 = { H:inner; int64:z; };
struct:HP = { H->:lp; int64:z; };
struct:W = { H->:hp; int64:z; };
struct:S = { int64:a; int64:b; };
struct:PE = { int64->:p; int64:z; };
enum:EK = { A(int64->); Z; };
func:rdk = int64(EK:e) never fails { int64:v = 0i64; pick (e) { (EK.A(q)) { v = <-q; }, (EK.Z) { v = 0i64; } } pass v; };
func:mk = List<int64>() never fails { List<int64>:l = raw list_init::<int64>(1i64); drop list_push(@l, 1i64); pass l; };
func:mkh = H() never fails { pass H{ l: raw mk(), k: 2i64, kk: K{ k: 3i64 } }; };
func:growh = int64(H->:w) never fails { loop(0i64, 64i64, 1i64) { drop list_push(@w.l, 1i64); } pass 7i64; };
func:fp = int64->(H->:q) never fails { pass @((<-q).l[0i64]); };
func:fpl = int64->(List<int64>->:q) never fails { pass @((<-q)[0i64]); };
func:fa = int64->(S->:q) never fails { pass @((<-q).a); };
func:idp = int64->(int64->:q) never fails { pass q; };
func:idh = H->(H->:q) never fails { pass q; };
func:fp2 = int64->(int64:v, H->:q) never fails { pass @((<-q).l[0i64]); };
func:fp2b = int64->(H->:q, int64:v) never fails { pass @((<-q).l[0i64]); };
func:rd2 = int64(int64->:e, int64:z) never fails { pass <-e; };
impl:H = { func:first = int64->(H->:self) never fails { pass @((<-self).l[0i64]); }; };
impl:K = { func:sel = int64->(K:self, H->:q) never fails { pass @((<-q).l[0i64]); }; };
func:g = int64(H->[2]:pa, H->:hz, int64:n) never fails {
    int64->:e = raw fp(hz);
    for (H->:m in pa) {
        e = raw fp(m);
    discard(raw growh(m));
    }
    pass (<-e) + 6i64;
};
func:f = int64(int64:n) never fails {
    H:h0 = raw mkh();
    H:h1 = raw mkh();
    H:hz = raw mkh();
    H->[2]:arr = [@h0, @h1];
    pass raw g(arr, @hz, n);
};
};
mod:yf2e_for_param_array = {
error:E5_yf2e_for_param_array;
struct:K = { int64:k; };
struct:H = { List<int64>:l; int64:k; K:kk; };
struct:H2 = { H:inner; int64:z; };
struct:HP = { H->:lp; int64:z; };
struct:W = { H->:hp; int64:z; };
struct:S = { int64:a; int64:b; };
struct:PE = { int64->:p; int64:z; };
enum:EK = { A(int64->); Z; };
func:rdk = int64(EK:e) never fails { int64:v = 0i64; pick (e) { (EK.A(q)) { v = <-q; }, (EK.Z) { v = 0i64; } } pass v; };
func:mk = List<int64>() never fails { List<int64>:l = raw list_init::<int64>(1i64); drop list_push(@l, 1i64); pass l; };
